e/pub/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 37\nL0: # cite0†Conor†www.strictlypositive.org 's Publications\nL1: \nL2: and other stories\nL3: \nL4: * * *\nL5: ## Drafts\nL6:   * cite1†Outrageous but Meaningful Coincidences , being my attempt to get my own show in Baltimore by implementing a small dependent type theory in Agda.\nL7:   * cite2†Image cite3†Ornamental Algebras, Algebraic Ornaments , tout seul. This paper explains how to formalize an overlay from my cite4†millenial propaganda†www.strictlypositive.org talk, or it would do if it was in any shape. The cite5†code does the business, though. The idea is to encode how one datatype can be built from another by a combination of refinement and annotation. By doing so, we expose coincidences as the consequences of design choices and gain the coresponding functionality for free.\nL8:   * cite6†Why walk when you can take the tube? , with Lucas Dixon and Peter Hancock. Lucas said we should compress open terms, speeding up access to the variables. I said a tube is what you get when you line up holes in a sequence, so Tube F = List (∂F 0). Hank said you can explore a structure by collecting the ways to move the cursor down : F → F ⋅ (∂F × I). This cite7†draft is very drafty: code one wet Sunday in Aberystwyth, 2006; formatting, section headings, and chat on the train back to Nottingham.\nL9: ## What this page used to say\nL10: \nL11: cite8†.bib†www.strictlypositive.org L12:   * cite9†Eliminating Dependent Pattern Matching†www.strictlypositive.org with cite10†Healfdene Goguen†healf.blogspot.com and cite11†James McKinna†www.dcs.st-and.ac.uk proves the reducibility of dependent pattern matching to old-fashioned eliminators and heterogeneous equality. In particular, we show how to formulate reduction rules which actually make sense, and that the translated programs preserve this computational behaviour.\nL13:   * cite12†Exploring the Regular Tree Types†www.strictlypositive.org with cite13†Peter Morris†www.cs.nott.ac.uk and cite14†Thorsten Altenkirch†www.cs.nott.ac.uk . Draft.\nL14:   * cite15†A Few Constructions on Constructors†www.strictlypositive.org with cite10†Healf Goguen†healf.blogspot.com and cite11†James McKinna†www.dcs.st-and.ac.uk . Draft.\nL15:   * cite16†Why Dependent Types Matter†www.strictlypositive.org with cite14†Thorsten Altenkirch†www.cs.nott.ac.uk and cite11†James McKinna†www.dcs.st-and.ac.uk . Submitted to ICFP 2005.\nL16:   * my cite17†AFP notes†www.strictlypositive.org on practical dependently typoed programming in Epigram. In colour. Shortly to appear in an LNCS volume.\nL17:   * cite18†I am not a number: I am a free variable†www.strictlypositive.org with cite11†James McKinna†www.dcs.st-and.ac.uk . This is the first instalment of how-to-implement-Epigram, but it's generally useful stuff if you're hacking around with syntax. Presented at the Haskell Workshop 2004, in cite19†Utah†www.strictlypositive.org , but not by either of us: thanks to Thomas, no thanks to KLM.\n"
